    "originalTitleEn": "ENGINEER PRO STUDENTS' BREAKTHROUGH PATH TO CONQUER GRAB",
    "originalHtmlEn": "<h2>ENGINEER PRO STUDENTS' BREAKTHROUGH PATH TO CONQUER GRAB  </h2><p>In the journey to conquer positions at Big Tech, each candidate has their own efforts and determination. Taking this opportunity, Engineer Pro will help you better understand the journey of an EP team student who successfully overcame difficult challenges to receive the opportunity to work at Grab.  </p><p>Join Engineer Pro to listen to profound sharing from EP students in the conversation below!</p><h2>Can you share with Engineer Pro a little about what led you to Grab?</h2><p>Engineer Pro Student: I started this journey when I decided to end my current job. At that time, I really wanted to find a new environment where I could develop more opportunities to develop professional skills and company culture. During the search process, I saw that Grab is recruiting positions that match my skills and desires. Therefore, I decided to submit my application and begin the process of carefully preparing for this interview.</p><h2>When you knew you wanted to apply for Grab, how did you prepare?</h2><p>Engineer Pro Student: After deciding to apply, I first started by completing my CV. Writing a CV is the first important factor for employers to pay attention to, so I participated in livestream sessions on writing and editing CVs from the Engineer Pro team. Thereby, learn how to highlight personal experiences and projects to suit the requirements of a large company like Grab. Along with that, I spent about 6 months rehearsing interview skills. This time, I focus on two main areas: algorithms and system design.</p><p>Regarding algorithms, regularly practice on platforms such as LeetCode, HackerRank, etc. Start with basic lessons and gradually improve, mainly the types of problems that often appear in interviews of large companies.  </p><p>With system design, spend a lot of time reading books, watching lectures, and learning about actual system architectures that you may encounter during the interview.</p><h2>So during the interview process, what rounds did you go through? Which round did you find most challenging?</h2><p>Engineer Pro Student: I went through three main rounds of interviews.  </p><ul><li>The first round is about the algorithm, divided into two phases. First, the interviewer will ask a few questions for about 15 minutes about the projects you have done, what projects have been challenging, or is there any recent technology that you have learned and found interesting, or is there anything you have recently done that you feel interested in? Next, we will start 2 algorithm code lessons within an hour. Each problem has moderate difficulty but requires doing it quickly and accurately. At first it was a bit stressful, but thanks to careful preparation, I was able to get through it.</li><li>Round two is about system design, this is the round I find the most difficult. They present a real case study and ask for solutions for those cases. In addition, each treatment method needs to be clearly explained, plus there is a bit of pressure, so in this round I felt \"burdened\" by in-depth questions from the interviewer. After that, the interviewer will have other additional questions such as database, networking, go, ...</li><li>The final round is the interview with the Manager about corporate culture, this round is much more comfortable. They mainly ask about previous work experience, how to handle problematic situations, and consider their compatibility with the company culture. This is the part that I feel more comfortable with, because I don't need to focus too much on expertise.</li></ul><h2>When you started working at Grab, how did you feel about the working environment? Compared to the previous company, is there any difference?</h2><p>Engineer Pro Student: Grab has a working environment that I really like. First, the culture here is very open and comfortable. There is not too much pressure on deadlines like in the old company. Everyone at Grab is always ready to help each other, regardless of whether you are new or have been working for a long time. This creates a pleasant working atmosphere where it is possible to both learn and grow without feeling stressed.</p><p>Another thing I appreciate is that Grab encourages employees to spend time researching in-depth about new technologies. Having more free time to learn more about the fields that interest me, something I didn't have much of a chance to do at my old company because I always had to chase tight deadlines.</p><h2>During the interview process at Grab, was there any situation that made you feel impressed or unforgettable?</h2><p>Engineer Pro Student: There was an interesting moment that I will always remember. When in the second round, the Interviewer realized I was nervous so he asked, \"Do you feel pressured?\", instead of answering seriously, I joked, \"I can sit with you until the evening for the interview because I already have the opportunity to interview, so if you have time, I can sit and do nothing.\" This makes the atmosphere of the interview more comfortable and I see the recruiters also smiling happily. This is a good way to break interview tension and make the conversation more pleasant.</p><h2>So after that interview process, how long does it take for you to receive an official offer letter from Grab?</h2><p>After the process of consideration and salary negotiation between the two sides, about 3-4 weeks I received the official offer letter for the position I applied for.</p><h2>What advice can you share for those preparing for interviews, especially those without much experience?</h2><p>Engineer Pro Student: The most important thing is confidence. When going for an interview, it's easy to get nervous, especially when faced with difficult questions. But if you are confident in yourself and your skills, you will perform much better. This not only helps you answer questions fluently but also leaves a good impression on the employer. I used to be very nervous in previous interviews, but after practicing and participating in many practice interviews, I have improved my confidence.</p><p>In addition, I want to say that although qualifications are important, practical skills are the deciding factor. When working, employers will pay more attention to your problem-solving ability and practical knowledge, rather than just looking at your degree. Therefore, focus on developing your skills and building your personal portfolio with real-life projects along with learning additional related certificates to have more advancement opportunities in the future.</p><h2>Thank you very much for your sincere and useful shares. Is there anything else you would like to say about your current job and the importance of learning outside of university?</h2><p>Engineer Pro students: I want to remind you that learning is a process that never stops, even after graduation. The knowledge learned from real work is sometimes more important than what you learn in school. If you have the opportunity, participate in courses, workshops and look for real projects to enrich your experience. This is the best way to advance in your career.</p><p>Engineer Pro thank you very much. Wishing you continued success at Grab and many achievements in the future!</p>",
